# The Championship desk. Must come BEFORE the catch-all, which would
# otherwise serve the Premier League app for every path.
/eflc         /eflc.html    200
/championship /eflc.html    200
# The La Liga desk. Same rule — before the catch-all.
/laliga       /laliga.html  200
/la-liga      /laliga.html  200
# THE HOMEPAGE IS TODAY'S MATCHES. `/` used to be the Premier League desk;
# it now serves the same-day card across every division, riskiest first. The
# desk moved to /pl and keeps a spelled-out alias.
#
# Both of these serve today.html, and that is deliberate rather than lazy: the
# two routes are two views of one dataset, and the pricing, the player-name
# join and the card builders behind them must not exist twice. The page reads
# its own path to decide which view it opened on. See the ROUTE comment there.
#
# `200!`, AND THE BANG IS WHAT MAKES IT WORK. Netlify serves a matching static
# file BEFORE it consults redirects, and `index.html` exists at the publish
# root, so `/` resolves to it as the directory index and an unforced rule here
# is silently ignored. That shipped: `/` kept serving the Premier League desk,
# so tapping "Today's matches" moved you from the PL desk to the PL desk and
# looked like a dead link.
# Invisible to any local server that applies these rules directly — the
# precedence is Netlify's, not the file's.
/             /today.html   200!
# The whole season, every date, plus the week's nine-fold.
/today        /today.html   200
/season       /today.html   200

# EVERY ACCA, IN ONE PLACE. A third view of the same file for the same reason
# the first two are: the day's acca, the week's nine-fold, the doubles and
# trebles and the settled record are all built from these rows, and a separate
# accas.html would have forked the pricing and both card builders to show them.
# They were scattered across two routes and a share card, and nobody could find
# them.
/accas        /today.html   200

# EVERY DERBY, ALL THREE DIVISIONS, IN DATE ORDER. A fourth view of the same
# file, for the same reason: which fixtures are derbies was written down in
# four separate places, each visible only to its own page, so nothing could
# draw the set across the divisions. The pairs are one list in assets/core.js
# now and this is the view of it. Same fixtures, same pricing, same frames as
# the routes above — a separate derbies.html would have forked all three to
# show a list.
/derbies      /today.html   200
/record       /today.html   200

# The Premier League desk, moved off `/`. Its own internal routing is entirely
# hash-based (#panel-...), so nothing inside it cares which path it is served
# from — only the league bar's self-link had to change.
/pl             /index.html   200
/premier-league /index.html   200

# OPTIONAL DATA FILES MUST 404, NOT FALL THROUGH.
#
# Three script tags carry `onerror="void 0"` for files that legitimately may
# not exist yet — data/lineups.js above all, which only appears once a harvest
# has landed a team sheet. That guard is worthless without this rule. Netlify
# serves static files ahead of redirects, so a file that EXISTS is unaffected;
# a file that does not existed used to fall through to the catch-all below and
# come back as index.html with HTTP 200. The browser never fires `error` on a
# 200 — it fires `SyntaxError: Unexpected token '<'` while parsing HTML as
# JavaScript, on every page load of all four desks.
#
# Found by serving the tree through these rules and reading the console.
# `python3 -m http.server` returns a real 404 for a missing file, so every
# earlier local run had the guard working and the bug invisible.
/data/*   /data-not-found.txt   404

/*    /index.html   200
